course: dictionary
lesson: 12124
title: Burning and combustion
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Combustion is the scientific word for burning. When a fuel joins with oxygen from the air, it reacts and gives out heat and light. A fire is combustion you can see.
explain: Every fire needs three things, called the fire triangle: fuel, oxygen and heat. «The wood would not catch fire until we struck the match to give it heat.» Take away any one side of the triangle and the fire goes out. That is why a lid on a pan puts out a cooking fire.
explain: Combustion is also used in machines. An internal combustion engine burns fuel inside itself, so a car engine uses combustion to move the car. Gas cookers burn gas in the same way, to make heat for cooking.
fact: Combustion means burning.
fact: Combustion is a chemical reaction between a fuel and oxygen.
fact: Combustion gives out heat and light, and often makes new substances such as carbon dioxide and water.
fact: The fire triangle shows that fuel, oxygen and heat must all be present for a fire to burn.
fact: «An internal combustion engine» burns fuel inside the engine, like a car engine.
exercise: What is combustion? | A type of rock | Dissolving sugar in a cup of tea | Freezing water into ice | *Burning, a reaction of a fuel with oxygen that gives out heat and light
exercise: The fire triangle has three sides: fuel, oxygen and what else? | Water | *Heat | Metal | Sand
exercise: «The fire went out when Sam put a lid on the pan, because the oxygen could not get in.» Which side of the fire triangle was taken away? | Light | Fuel | Heat | *Oxygen
test: In a car, what is an internal combustion engine? | An engine made of wood | A type of battery | *An engine that burns fuel inside itself to make it move | An engine that runs only on water
test: «Cutting off the wood from the fire stopped it burning.» Which side of the fire triangle was taken away? | Oxygen | *Fuel | Heat | Light
exam: Why does combustion need oxygen, and what happens to a fire if the oxygen is taken away? | *Combustion needs oxygen to react with the fuel, so the fire goes out | Oxygen is a fuel, so the fire gets bigger | Oxygen only makes smoke, not fire | Oxygen makes fire colder, so it stops
